Subject: [PATCH 2/7] decoder: clamp []byte / string allocation against forged length

Decoder.bytes (used for decoding []byte) and Decoder.bytesPtr (used
for *[]byte and the string fast path that takes ownership of the
underlying buffer) called

    readN(d.r, b, n)

with n taken directly from the wire-format bin8/16/32 or
str8/16/32 length. readN allocates the entire n-byte slice up front
before reading. A hostile bin32/str32 header (for example,
0xc6 0xff 0xff 0xff 0xff followed by zero or a few bytes) was enough
to make the decoder request roughly 4 GiB before the short input
failed, which is a reliable single-packet OOM against any service
that unmarshals untrusted MessagePack.

The decoder already has a growth-capped reader (readNGrow) that
allocates in bytesAllocLimit-sized chunks as actual bytes arrive.
This patch routes both bytes and bytesPtr through readNGrow unless
the caller has explicitly opted out via Decoder.DisableAllocLimit(true).

Backwards compatible: same exported API, same wire format. Output
bytes/strings are byte-for-byte identical for well-formed input.

Apply from the package root:

    patch -p1 < 0002-decoder-clamp-bytes-and-string-allocation.patch

---
 decode_string.go | 18 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/decode_string.go
+++ b/decode_string.go
@@ -92,7 +92,16 @@
 	if n == -1 {
 		return nil, nil
 	}
-	return readN(d.r, b, n)
+	// Use the growth-capped reader unless limits have been explicitly
+	// disabled. Without the cap, a hostile bin32 / str32 length (for
+	// example, 0xc6 0xff 0xff 0xff 0xff) tricks the decoder into
+	// allocating a multi-gigabyte slice up front before the underlying
+	// short input fails. With the cap, allocation grows in
+	// bytesAllocLimit-sized chunks only as actual bytes arrive.
+	if d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag != 0 {
+		return readN(d.r, b, n)
+	}
+	return readNGrow(d.r, b, n)
 }
 
 func (d *Decoder) decodeStringTemp() (string, error) {
@@ -139,7 +148,14 @@
 		return nil
 	}
 
-	*ptr, err = readN(d.r, *ptr, n)
+	// See bytes(): route through the growth-capped reader unless limits
+	// have been explicitly disabled, so a forged bin32 / str32 length
+	// cannot trigger a multi-gigabyte up-front allocation.
+	if d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag != 0 {
+		*ptr, err = readN(d.r, *ptr, n)
+	} else {
+		*ptr, err = readNGrow(d.r, *ptr, n)
+	}
 	return err
 }
 
